# EXAMPLE — QMS sink DNS drop-in for MTTL-W01.  ADDITIVE, exact hosts only.
#
# This is a template. Replace <RELAY_HOST_IP> with the relay host IP, then install
# it as a NEW, SEPARATE file  /etc/dnsmasq.d/mttl-qms-sink.conf  via:
#     deploy/proxmox-lxc/dns/apply_qms_sink_dns.sh
# and remove it with:
#     deploy/proxmox-lxc/dns/rollback_qms_sink_dns.sh
# It NEVER edits the existing /etc/dnsmasq.d/mttl-relay.conf (mef/brk2 redirect).
#
# Effect: the two QMS diagnostic-log upload hosts resolve to the local relay so
# that if the firmware makes a QMS HTTPS attempt it lands on rule
# relay/rules/16-qms-sink.py and is recorded — instead of being silently
# blackholed by the network egress block.
#
#   log.toi.ommeq.com      — V1.0.105 QMS endpoint (POST /read_iot_wifi :443)
#   hdslog.lguplus.co.kr   — seen in a separate 1.0.66 runtime note; same purpose,
#                            NOT assumed to be the same service
#
# NOT redirected — left exactly as they are:
#   dev.toi.ommeq.com   (its containment / real-IP resolution is unchanged)
#   toi.ommeq.com , *.ommeq.com , mef.onem2m.uplus.co.kr , brk2.onem2m.uplus.co.kr ,
#   lguplus.co.kr , *.lguplus.co.kr , and every other name.
#
# No wildcard. dnsmasq's `address=/h/ip` also covers sub-labels of h (e.g.
# *.log.toi.ommeq.com) — there are none in play, and it does NOT match the parent
# (toi.ommeq.com) or a sibling (dev.toi.ommeq.com). Same idiom as mttl-relay.conf.
#
# This does NOT re-open any cloud / OTA / version-check / Voltra path: the relay
# is decloud (smartrelay.toml has no `dns=`, so handle_tls has no upstream) and
# 16-qms-sink.py never forwards. Keep the network egress block in place.

address=/log.toi.ommeq.com/<RELAY_HOST_IP>
address=/hdslog.lguplus.co.kr/<RELAY_HOST_IP>
